From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:42:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: edit, delete, groups, places, contact cards and
 mentions from the app
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

* Edit, delete, groups, places, contact cards and mentions from the app

The webOS Messaging app can now do what WhatsApp does besides sending a
text. The transport (imlibpurpleservice) brings it in two ways.

Edits, deletes and groups come as signals the transport emits for every
account: webos-im-send-edit, webos-im-send-delete, webos-im-group-create,
webos-im-group-invite and webos-im-group-leave. The handlers in
glue/login.c take those for a WhatsApp account and return TRUE, which
tells the transport the command was done; webos_actions.go sends them:
an edit of our own message (BuildEdit), a delete for everyone
(BuildRevoke), a new group (it is put on the buddy list and a line in it
opens the chat), new members, and leaving. What fails is said in the
chat.

A place, a contact card and mentions come with an outgoing message, on
the conversation for the length of the send (webos-send-location,
-contact, -mentions); send_im and send_chat pass them on, and
send_text_message sends a location pin or a contact card instead of the
text, and turns each "@Name" the app wrote into WhatsApp's "@<number>"
with the contact in MentionedJID. The text the app wrote is still what
is echoed and reported as the outbox id, which the app matches on. The
message context - its expiry, the quoted message, the mentions - now
goes with whichever kind is sent. The parsing is in webos_send.go, with
tests.

* Answer a chat WhatsApp addresses by LID at its LID

Neither our typing nor our read receipts showed on the other phone
(a Pixel 3a, 2026-11-07). The chats come addressed by LID - typing
arrives as <chatstate from="...@lid"> - and the plugin answered at the
phone number: send_typing sent <chatstate to="...@s.whatsapp.net">, and
a received message's read receipt was kept with its chat and sender
after lidToPn, so it went to the phone number too.

Send the typing state to the contact's LID when the store knows it
(handler.pnToLid). Keep a read receipt under the chat's phone number,
which is the conversation's name and so what reading the chat gives,
but send it to the chat and sender as the message came.

* Say why a group did not take someone

Adding people to a group answered with WhatsApp's own codes, as in
"Could not add 31611111111 to the group (error 409)". It came after
leaving a group made here and then adding ourselves back
(2026-11-07): once left, we are no member of it any more.

Say what the codes mean, as other WhatsApp clients document them
(Baileys: 409 already in the group, 403 their privacy settings, with
an invite to send them when WhatsApp made one): "is already in the
group", "can only be added by an invite", "their privacy settings do
not allow it". A refusal of the whole change with 401 or 403
(whatsmeow ErrIQNotAuthorized, ErrIQForbidden) says we are no longer
in the group, or not one of its admins. Any other code is shown as
before.

* Show a mention by the name of who it mentions

A mention someone else sends showed as "@31611111111": the plugin put
the buddy alias in place of the number, and purple's alias for anyone
not on the buddy list - most of a group's members - is the JID itself.

Take the alias when there is a real one, else the names WhatsApp's
contact store has for them (full, first, business, push name, as the
display name takes them), and the number only when nobody knows it.
Replace "@<number>" only, not the same digits elsewhere in the text or
a longer number that starts with them.

* Remove someone from a WhatsApp group

The Messaging app can now remove people from a group, through the
transport's webos-im-group-remove signal (account, group, members
joined by "\x1f"), as it adds them with webos-im-group-invite. Each
member is a phone JID, a LID or a +E.164 number, as the group's member
list has them. group_remove asks WhatsApp to remove them
(UpdateGroupParticipants, ParticipantChangeRemove) on a goroutine, and
says in the group what failed: the whole change, when we are no longer
in the group or not one of its admins (401/403), or one person, with
the code WhatsApp gave for them. When anyone was removed the group's
member list is read again, so the chat's people are current.

group_change_failed now takes what was being done ("add to", "remove
from"); what an invite says is unchanged.

* Give a WhatsApp group member's phone number to the app

Group Info showed a WhatsApp group's members only by their address,
and a group WhatsApp addresses by LID gives every member as
"<id>@lid": no number anywhere, and two members of the same name
could not be told apart.

Keep each member's phone number on their chat buddy, as the chat
buddy attribute "phone" ("+<digits>"), for imlibpurpleservice's
getChatMembers to give the app. The number is the member's own JID
when they are given by number, else the one WhatsApp sent with the
member (GroupParticipant.PhoneNumber), else what the store's LID map
knows for their LID; a member whose number is not known has none.

The numbers go to the C side next to the participants, in their
order: in the group message (participant_phones) and from
gowhatsapp_go_query_group_participants, which gives them through a
new out parameter. gowhatsapp_chat_set_participants and
gowhatsapp_enter_group_chat take them; purple_update_group takes the
handler, to read the store.

* Say in a WhatsApp group who joined, left or was removed

A WhatsApp group's own news reached the plugin as events.GroupInfo, for
which handler.go had no case, so nothing of it showed in the chat:
someone removed from the group, here or on another phone, only left the
member list.

Each change is now a notice line in the group, as the WhatsApp app
writes it: "You removed Ann", "Ann added Ben and you", "Ben left", "Ben
joined", "Ben joined using this group's invite link", "Ann changed the
group name to “X”", "Ann changed the group description" (or deleted
it), "Ann made Ben an admin", "Ben is no longer an admin". We are "You"
by our phone number or our LID. Others are named as a mention names
them, the buddy alias or the names WhatsApp's contact store has, with a
LID looked up as its number; else "+<digits>", and "Unknown user" for a
LID whose number is not known, never the LID itself.

The lines are system lines, as tdlib-purple writes a Telegram group's
news, so the transport keeps them in the thread without a banner. They
carry the time of the change (purple_display_system_message_at), and
the glue treats them as it treats an old message: one from before we
connected is dropped when "discard old messages" is on, else written
with its own time. Other system lines have no time and are unchanged.

When members, admins or the name changed, the group is read again
(GetGroupInfo, purple_update_group) once, two seconds after the last
change, so Group Info is current and a burst of changes on connecting
does not read it once for each. Not when we are the one who left or
was removed: the group no longer answers us.

* Say in a WhatsApp group when someone shared recent messages

A group member can share the group's recent messages with people newly
added to it. The group then gets a MessageHistoryNotice, which the
WhatsApp app shows to everyone, and the new members a
MessageHistoryBundle holding the messages (as media). Neither carries
text or an attachment handle_message knew, so nothing was shown for
either: the message was only cached and its read receipt kept.

The notice is now a notice line in the group, a system line with the
message's time, as a group's own news is (group_events.go): "Ann shared
12 recent messages with Ben and Cara", "You shared 1 recent message with
Ben", "Ann shared recent messages with you" when no count is given, "Ann
shared 5 recent messages" when no receivers are, and "Ann shared 9
recent messages with Ben, Cara and 2 others" for more than three ("you,
Ben and 2 others" when we are one of them). People are named as the
group notices name them: "You"/"you" by our number or LID, else the
buddy alias or WhatsApp's contact names, else "+<digits>", "Unknown
user" for a LID whose number is not known.

The bundle is dropped, with one debug line: the messages in it are not
read, and there is nothing to show for it.

* Rename a WhatsApp group from the Messaging app

The Messaging app can now give a group a new name, through the
transport's webos-im-group-rename signal (account, group, name), as it
creates groups and adds and removes people. The signal is FALSE, so the
transport says it failed, for another prpl's account, a chat this
account does not know (not in the buddy list, not open here), a chat
that is no group JID, or a name that is empty once trimmed.

group_rename asks WhatsApp for it (SetGroupName) on a goroutine. The
name goes as the app sent it, trimmed: it is not cut to a length.
whatsmeow says group names are limited to 25 characters and a longer
one is refused with 406 (ReqCreateGroup), but that is WhatsApp's to
enforce, and a name cut here would be one nobody typed. What failed is
said in the group: "you are no longer in it, or not one of its admins"
for 401/403, "WhatsApp did not accept the name. It may be too long."
for 406, else the error. The log names the group, never the name.

When WhatsApp took it, the chat is named again here at once. WhatsApp's
own news of the change (events.GroupInfo) brings the line "You changed
the group name to “X”" (group_events.go), and now also names the chat
before that line is written, so the line, and the app's thread with it,
comes under the new name; a rename on another phone or by another
member does the same.

A group's new name reaches the glue as a group without members
(purple_rename_group): the buddy list chat is aliased, the members,
the room list and auto-join are left alone. Any group update now also
sets an open conversation's title from the buddy list's name
(purple_conversation_autoset_title), which libpurple does not do when
a chat is aliased.

* Show a received WhatsApp mention in bold

Signal and Telegram show a mention of someone as a bold "@Name"; a
WhatsApp one was plain. The text is plain until it is HTML-escaped
just before it is shown, so replace_mention marks the "@Name" with
two control characters that escaping leaves alone, and they become
<b> and </b> after it. A sender's own marks are dropped first, and an
edit, which is not shown as HTML, keeps the plain "@Name".

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
